WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.007 --> 00:00:02.650
- You know The Foot, that roll, it's call Foot.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:03.483
You never had one?

00:00:03.483 --> 00:00:04.372
- No, no, no.
- It's a green package right?

00:00:04.372 --> 00:00:05.552
^- I know what you're talking about.

00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:07.325
^(speaking foreign language)

00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:09.858
- Favorite snack, I was like, whoa!

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.100
- I never ate it like that.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.363
Just rip everything off and like punch it up into this like,

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.177
I don't have time for this shit.

00:00:16.177 --> 00:00:18.677
(light music)

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.590
- Today I'm here with a couple of

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.300
my Korean American friends,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.900
and we're gonna be talking about how we grew up in America

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.390
^as Korean immigrants, or international students.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.000
^- I was born in Korea, Seoul.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.060
^I moved to Diamond Bar, D.B.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.600
^Went to college in Boston,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.780
^and then moved back to Diamond Bar.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:36.740
^- I was born in Korea,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.500
^so I moved out to America in 2002,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.230
^and then I came to California.

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.640
^Ever since then, I just, I just stayed here.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.940
^- I came here in 2002 with my family.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.490
^My first place in the United States was Waikiki.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.220
^After Hawaii, I got L.A., O.C., the Fullerton.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.600
^Now I'm living right next to the La Christina,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.433
^- [Evan] Basically California (mumbles).

00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:56.440
^but, yeah, yeah, yeah, right.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.988
^- I was born in Korea, 2002,

00:00:57.988 --> 00:01:01.610
^our family work decided to move to New Zealand first.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.580
^2006, I moved to the sin city, Las Vegas.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.683
^And then I moved to Idaho for Boise City University.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.120
^- And then you've been living here.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.342
^- For almost a year.

00:01:11.342 --> 00:01:14.002
(plucky string music)

00:01:14.002 --> 00:01:15.425
^- I don't think I've ever talked about this.

00:01:15.425 --> 00:01:19.092
^(speaking foreign language)

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.082
^(laughs)

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:25.749
^(speaking foreign language)

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:37.632
^(laughs)

00:01:37.632 --> 00:01:41.382
^(speaking foreign language)

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.699
^- Thanks to social media,

00:01:47.699 --> 00:01:51.366
^(speaking foreign language)

00:01:52.197 --> 00:01:53.783
^- I'm an only child, right.

00:01:53.793 --> 00:01:54.626
^(speaking foreign language)

00:01:54.626 --> 00:01:55.945
^like a brother and a sister.

00:01:55.945 --> 00:01:59.612
^(speaking foreign language)

00:02:15.904 --> 00:02:17.278
^(all exclaim)

00:02:17.278 --> 00:02:18.322
^- That was a thing.

00:02:18.322 --> 00:02:21.989
^(speaking foreign language)

00:02:25.114 --> 00:02:26.169
^(laughs)

00:02:26.169 --> 00:02:29.836
^(speaking foreign language)

00:02:33.232 --> 00:02:36.149
(light drum music)

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.550
- My close group of friends, we do speak English,

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.730
but, it's like back and forth, Korean and English.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.320
- Yeah, just like half, half, right?

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.915
We would say one sentence in English,

00:02:44.915 --> 00:02:46.420
and we would say the next sentence in Korean.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.690
Most of my guy friends are English-speaking friends,

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.130
but almost all female friends are Korean speakers.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:52.963
Which is interesting.

00:02:52.963 --> 00:02:55.910
- My friends, like, more than 70%, they use Korean.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:59.127
Some of them, they mix use, like one sentence Korean,

00:02:59.127 --> 00:03:00.720
one sentence in English.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.350
- The majority of my friends are English-only.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.950
But, I think ever since I moved into California,

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:09.810
it's more both cultures of Korean and America.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.660
^- You guys all do speak Korean in your households, right?

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:16.337
^(speaking foreign language)

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.100
^- Hi, Mrs. Lim!

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:22.993
(laughs)

00:03:22.993 --> 00:03:25.576
(groovy music)

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.130
- I listened to a lot of music.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:27.963
- Hip-hop.

00:03:27.963 --> 00:03:29.713
^- [Evan] I like 50 Cent and The Game.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.240
- I think I was more like an R&amp;B.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.390
^- Oh, Omarion.
- Whoo!

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.406
^- [Danny] Omarion, Chris Brown.

00:03:34.406 --> 00:03:35.768
^(speaking foreign language)

00:03:35.768 --> 00:03:36.601
- B2K.

00:03:37.201 --> 00:03:39.250
- Bom, bom, bom, that song, right?

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.450
- So I picked up a lot of slang,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:41.890
so a lot of kids make fun of me.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.010
You have to learn English first, and then learn slang.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.720
- So you just dropped like F-bombs?

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:46.963
- No, I didn't curse.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.380
'Cause a lot of rap songs,

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.630
grammatically it doesn't make sense,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:51.770
you know what I mean, right?

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:53.960
TV shows, Friends, Prince of Bel-Air.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.029
- I don't watch any of that shit.

00:03:55.029 --> 00:03:55.862
- Pokemon.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.263
- [All] Pokemon!

00:03:57.798 --> 00:03:59.780
- My age was 18 right,

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.940
so I had to make some cash at that moment,

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.250
so like, I got a job.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.330
Coffee place, like a Starbucks in the mall.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:09.913
I need to get orders from customers, right.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.810
But my English was really, really short.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.167
So this one lady came up, and they're like,

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:15.013
"No, Asian boy, listen up," you know,

00:04:15.013 --> 00:04:16.817
and like she keep (mumbles) again and again,

00:04:16.817 --> 00:04:18.019
and I'm like, I need to write it down.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:18.995
- Bring that lady right here.

00:04:18.995 --> 00:04:20.051
(laughs)

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.551
- I remember your face, still.

00:04:21.629 --> 00:04:22.570
- Yeah, that must have helped you a lot,

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.740
I mean, especially working.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.267
- It was really hard.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.543
Many nights I cried.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:29.243
Sorry (laughs).

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.360
- In elementary school when I first moved,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.676
I wanted to get a Gatorade,

00:04:33.676 --> 00:04:35.703
but I couldn't pronounce it, like how I just said it.

00:04:35.703 --> 00:04:36.690
I was like, Getorade.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.281
Hi, can I get a Geterade?

00:04:38.281 --> 00:04:39.500
(laughs)

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.380
The lady who was working at the cafeteria,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.256
she wasn't really fluent in English either.

00:04:43.256 --> 00:04:44.089
She was like, what?

00:04:44.089 --> 00:04:45.080
She couldn't get it, so I couldn't buy it.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.590
I remember just going to drink like, fountain water.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:48.423
Sadness.

00:04:48.423 --> 00:04:50.530
(upbeat xylophone music)

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:51.910
- I think in both languages, actually.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.030
- I kind of understand,

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.810
but I don't think a lot of viewers who understand...

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.933
- [Evan] Wait, what, there's no way!

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.460
Korean or--

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.293
- I don't think I think that much,

00:05:00.293 --> 00:05:02.054
I can't say this, I don't know.

00:05:02.054 --> 00:05:03.060
- [Evan] Here's a good one,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.910
do you count in English or in Korean?

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.176
- One, no in English.

00:05:06.176 --> 00:05:07.009
- English, english?

00:05:07.009 --> 00:05:08.040
I count in Korean.
- [Danny] Oh, really?

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.574
- Yeah, it's a lot faster.

00:05:09.574 --> 00:05:11.020
(speaking foreign language)

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.334
instead of one, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:14.000
- Oh, really, you do it in Korean?

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.100
- Whenever I jot down numbers, I always think in Korean.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.013
- [Danny] Oh, really?

00:05:17.276 --> 00:05:18.570
No, I process it in English.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.000
- As you speak, I can understand both languages in my mind.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.663
- Right now, I'm thinking in English.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:25.763
- Okay.

00:05:26.019 --> 00:05:27.442
(laughs)

00:05:27.442 --> 00:05:29.295
(light music)

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.110
- Where I grew up, people used to say

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.763
that Asians are minorities here,

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.590
but I've never really felt that way,

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.740
from middle school to high school

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.390
'cause about 80% were Asians.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:38.223
- Oh, wow.

00:05:38.223 --> 00:05:39.910
- Yeah, I was never really picked on,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.010
up until I went to Boston.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.970
Yeah, and then I was like the only Asian out of 400 kids,

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.620
and I was like, okay, this is a little weird.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.710
- For me, in middle school, in New Jersey,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.383
a lot of kids called me F.O.B., of course.

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:54.110
F.O.B. stands for fresh off the boat.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:55.740
It's a daily name that I just had to go through.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:57.570
Every time I go to school, it's like an insult,

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.567
you know, nobody wants to be called F.O.Bs, you know.

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.430
But right now it's okay, like I'm all grown up,

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:02.500
we can take it as a joke.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.660
But when you're like 12 or 13, it's traumatic.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.301
What I clearly remember is,

00:06:07.301 --> 00:06:09.730
there was this Korean guy in middle school.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.000
He spoke zero Korean,

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.010
but he made an effort to speak Korean

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.770
so that he could help throughout my school days.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:17.370
He saw other kids making fun of me.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:18.400
You know, you're supposed to protect me,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.093
but he like, started making fun of me with them.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.820
And he's like joining them to make fun of me.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:24.653
^So like that--

00:06:24.653 --> 00:06:25.681
^(speaking foreign language)

00:06:25.681 --> 00:06:27.230
^(laughs)

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.090
I hope this doesn't happen anymore.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.800
- I think when I was in high school,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.830
people called me like High Definition.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:33.663
- Why is it high def?

00:06:33.663 --> 00:06:35.778
- 'Cause the widescreen, 'cause of the eyes.

00:06:35.778 --> 00:06:37.830
- No way.
- Dude, that is so messed up.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.800
- High definition...
- So messed up.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.870
- Something like, I just hope that watching this,

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:44.850
like they will learn not to say in those kind of manner.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.745
(light music)

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:47.720
- Wearing shoes inside.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.490
- Oh, yeah.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.430
- Do you guys realize how dirty your shoes are?

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.550
- You go everywhere wearing shoes,

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:57.485
so you have all that germ, and especially like carpets.

00:06:57.485 --> 00:06:58.395
- Yeah.

00:06:58.395 --> 00:07:01.021
It collects more dust, so it's even worse.

00:07:01.021 --> 00:07:03.580
- Carpet flooring is actually my other culture shock.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.450
I've never seen carpet flooring in Korea.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.721
Korean school, your class stays as it is,

00:07:07.721 --> 00:07:09.115
and then your teacher.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:10.120
- You have to move, yeah.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:12.760
- But in America, the students move, in America.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.633
So in the American school you have to make more friends.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.380
In Korea, you're stuck with them you're entire year.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:19.390
It was a good culture shock.

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:20.760
I was like, oh, this is cool.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.367
- For me, it's to pay tips after you eat something.

00:07:23.367 --> 00:07:24.410
- [All] Yes.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.603
- In Korea, there's no tip culture.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.553
- Hot Cheetos.

00:07:27.553 --> 00:07:29.410
Oh, my God, Cheetos, when I first had Hot Cheetos,

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.660
it was like party in mouth, I was like, whoa!

00:07:31.708 --> 00:07:32.791
What is this?

00:07:33.031 --> 00:07:35.533
(relaxing music)

00:07:35.533 --> 00:07:37.250
^(speaking foreign language)

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:38.610
- Of course you have a language barrier,

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.240
you know, English isn't your first language.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.720
I'm a very outgoing person as you can all see,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.090
but like, when I first moved here,

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:47.810
I got more introverted because I didn't know the language.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:48.643
I didn't know the culture,

00:07:48.643 --> 00:07:50.780
so I was just scared to talk to people.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:52.560
You just have to break out of that

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:53.720
and if you can't speak English,

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:54.553
it's not stress for you,

00:07:54.553 --> 00:07:57.506
it's stress for the person you're talking to. (laughs)

00:07:57.506 --> 00:07:58.339
If he doesn't understand, he's gonna get stressed,

00:07:58.339 --> 00:07:59.717
^but like, you just have to speak your mind.

00:07:59.717 --> 00:08:03.384
^(speaking foreign language)

00:08:10.631 --> 00:08:12.081
^(laughs)

00:08:12.081 --> 00:08:15.748
^(speaking foreign language)

00:08:21.185 --> 00:08:23.768
(bluesy music)

00:08:31.903 --> 00:08:34.372
(whooshing and squeaking)

